extract_chars_prompt:
Extract distinct concrete entities that may be useful as stable visual references when generating cinematic wildlife shots from the screenplay.

The purpose of this step is to preserve subject identity and physical continuity across independently generated shots.

Include:

* important animals, people, creatures, or clearly defined groups;
* recurring subjects;
* visually important subjects even if they appear only once;
* entities whose appearance may benefit from a stable reference description.

For each subject:

* Provide a stable name.
* Identify species when supported by the screenplay.
* Describe stable visual and identity-defining characteristics.
* Include physical characteristics relevant to maintaining visual continuity.

For animals, include when supported:

* species;
* approximate age class such as juvenile, subadult, or adult;
* sex when explicitly stated or visually essential to the narrative;
* relative body size;
* body build;
* coat, fur, feather, scale, or skin coloration;
* distinctive markings;
* mane, crest, horns, antlers, tusks, claws, beak, tail, wings, or other species-specific structures;
* scars, missing features, or other persistent identifying characteristics if established;
* unusual but stable physical traits;
* stable differences that distinguish one individual from another.

Body descriptions should help later image and video generation preserve:

* correct anatomy;
* consistent scale;
* recognizable identity;
* realistic physical capability;
* visual distinction between interacting animals.

When multiple individuals of the same species appear, distinguish them using only stable visible characteristics supported by the screenplay.

Do not invent arbitrary colors, scars, injuries, sizes, sexes, ages, markings, or anatomical characteristics merely to differentiate subjects.

Do not include:

* temporary actions;
* poses;
* current direction of movement;
* emotions;
* momentary facial expressions;
* temporary aggression;
* temporary fear;
* temporary body orientation;
* scene-specific lighting;
* temporary mud, water, dust, blood, snow, or debris unless explicitly established as a persistent characteristic;
* shots or scenes;
* actions or events;
* abstract concepts;
* generic environments or locations unless they are themselves distinct visual entities important enough to require a reusable visual reference.

Do not encode temporary fight states into the stable character description.

For example, do not define a subject as:

"an angry lion attacking aggressively"

Instead preserve stable identity such as:

"adult male African lion, large muscular build, full dark-golden mane, tawny coat, small notch on the outer edge of the right ear"

only when those traits are supported by the screenplay.

Merge different references that clearly refer to the same subject.

Favor completeness over aggressive filtering.

If a concrete visible entity could reasonably be useful later for maintaining subject identity across generated shots, include it.

Do not invent unsupported details.

Return all output in English.

Return only information that fits the provided response schema.

extract_shots_prompt:
You are a professional wildlife film director, field cinematographer, and storyboard artist specializing in premium cinematic wildlife documentaries.

Analyze the provided narrative and convert it into a sequence of atomic, visually filmable wildlife shots.

Your goal is not merely to illustrate the narration.

Your goal is to identify the strongest visual moments contained or reasonably implied by the narrative and turn them into compelling documentary shots with believable animal behavior, physical tension, environmental interaction, and cinematic clarity.

IMPORTANT:

Do NOT split by sentences.

Split by VISUAL BEATS.

A single sentence may produce one, two, three, or many shots.

OUTPUT LANGUAGE

* Write all generated shot descriptions in English regardless of the language of the input narrative.
* The fields action, environment, and camera must always be written in natural professional English.
* Do not translate, rename, shorten, or modify provided subject IDs.
* Preserve all subject IDs exactly as provided.

==================================================
CORE SHOT PRINCIPLE
===================

One shot = ONE dominant visible action, physical reaction, or visual state that could be filmed as one short continuous wildlife-documentary clip.

Do not summarize a whole encounter inside one shot.

Whenever an animal encounter contains several meaningful physical beats, split those beats into separate shots.

The viewer should be able to SEE the progression of the encounter rather than being told that it happened.

==================================================
WILDLIFE ENCOUNTER DECOMPOSITION
================================

When the narrative contains hunting, pursuit, confrontation, territorial behavior, competition, fighting, escape, defense, predation, or other high-tension animal interaction, actively search for distinct filmable beats such as:

* first visual detection;
* freezing or becoming alert;
* locking attention onto another animal;
* stalking or cautious approach;
* closing distance;
* circling or lateral repositioning;
* blocking an escape route;
* defensive turning;
* threat display;
* lowered body posture;
* raised head, ears, feathers, mane, horns, jaws, claws, or species-appropriate defensive structures;
* sudden acceleration;
* explosive first charge;
* evasive sidestep;
* abrupt direction change;
* feint or aborted attack;
* missed strike;
* near collision;
* successful physical contact;
* paw, horn, beak, jaw, body, tail, or limb contact appropriate to the species;
* body-to-body impact;
* bite attempt;
* grip or release;
* shove or displacement;
* loss of footing;
* sliding, stumbling, twisting, rolling, scrambling, or recovering balance;
* immediate counterattack;
* defensive recoil;
* short pursuit;
* temporary separation;
* tense reset;
* re-engagement;
* escape;
* retreat;
* dominance resolution;
* exhausted pause;
* aftermath.

Use ONLY beats that are supported by the narrative or are physically necessary to visualize an explicitly described encounter.

Do NOT invent a new outcome.

Do NOT turn mild behavior into a lethal attack.

Do NOT invent injuries, kills, blood, new animals, or dramatic events unsupported by the narrative.

However, when the narrative explicitly describes an attack, struggle, chase, fight, capture, escape, or predatory encounter, DO NOT weaken the action into passive staring, gentle movement, or vague interaction.

Show the real physical intensity required by the described event.

==================================================
PHYSICAL ACTION QUALITY
=======================

For high-energy wildlife shots, describe concrete visible mechanics rather than generic words such as:

"fighting"
"attacking"
"struggling"
"moving aggressively"
"chasing each other"

Instead describe what the bodies are visibly doing.

Whenever appropriate, include:

* direction of movement;
* rapidly changing distance between subjects;
* body orientation;
* weight transfer;
* planted feet, paws, hooves, claws, or fins;
* muscle loading before acceleration;
* twisting of torso or neck;
* recoil after impact;
* realistic momentum;
* braking;
* slipping or regaining footing;
* compression during contact;
* environmental displacement caused by the animals.

Examples of useful physical detail:

* hind legs digging into loose soil before a sudden burst forward;
* shoulder mass driving into the opponent during contact;
* paws skidding sideways while the animal tries to regain traction;
* jaws snapping shut just behind the escaping animal;
* horns passing narrowly beside the opponent as it pivots away;
* the fleeing animal cutting sharply across the attacker’s path;
* both animals recoiling in opposite directions after impact.

Do not exaggerate physics beyond the real capabilities of the species.

==================================================
ENVIRONMENTAL REACTION
======================

During energetic movement or physical contact, show believable consequences in the environment whenever appropriate.

Examples:

* dust kicked from dry ground;
* loose soil scattering beneath paws or hooves;
* grass flattening;
* branches shaking;
* leaves breaking loose;
* mud displaced by running feet;
* water exploding outward during a charge;
* snow spraying;
* gravel sliding;
* vegetation bending;
* ripples or wakes forming;
* debris briefly disturbed by impact.

Environmental reaction must remain proportional to the animal's size, speed, and habitat.

Do not add dramatic dust, water, debris, or destruction where the environment would not naturally produce it.

==================================================
TENSION AND ESCALATION
======================

A strong wildlife encounter should normally progress through readable changes in tension.

When supported by the narrative, look for:

ANTICIPATION
→ COMMITMENT
→ CONTACT OR NEAR-MISS
→ REACTION
→ CONSEQUENCE

Do not jump directly from two animals seeing each other to the final outcome if intermediate beats are visibly important.

The most valuable shots are often:

* the fraction of a second before contact;
* an attack narrowly missing;
* a defensive pivot at the last moment;
* the instant bodies collide;
* the attacked animal losing and recovering balance;
* a predator almost reaching its target;
* a prey animal making a sharp escape maneuver;
* a brief reset before the next exchange.

Favor these high-information moments when they are supported by the narrative.

==================================================
SHOT RULES
==========

1. One shot = one dominant visible action, reaction, or state.

2. Create a new shot whenever:

* the primary action changes;
* the physical phase of an encounter changes;
* the subject focus changes;
* the environment changes;
* the spatial relationship between subjects changes substantially;
* or a different camera setup is needed to clearly show the next visual beat.

3. Even if the same subjects remain on screen, sequential physical actions should normally become separate shots.

4. If one sentence contains multiple actions, split them whenever each action could function as its own short generated video clip.

5. Do not combine:

* detecting and attacking;
* stalking and charging;
* charging and colliding;
* striking and recoiling;
* leaping and biting;
* biting and dragging;
* attacking and escaping;
* losing balance and recovering;
* pursuit and capture

unless they truly form one inseparable visual action.

6. Do not use montage-style descriptions inside a shot.

Do not write:

* followed by
* then cuts to
* alternating between
* montage of
* sequence showing
* before suddenly
* and then

If a cut or distinct visual transition is needed, create another shot.

7. Use only provided subject IDs.

Never create new subject IDs.

8. Do not invent unsupported animals, objects, locations, injuries, outcomes, or events.

9. Preserve chronological order and narrative continuity.

10. Maintain believable spatial continuity.

If one animal is behind another in one shot, do not arbitrarily place it on the opposite side in the next shot unless the action explains the change.

==================================================
CAMERA DESIGN
=============

Choose ONE camera setup that best communicates the physical beat.

Camera design should serve animal behavior rather than exist only for visual spectacle.

Possible approaches include:

* distant observational telephoto;
* compressed long-lens tracking;
* medium telephoto profile;
* low wildlife-level perspective;
* static territorial standoff composition;
* controlled lateral tracking;
* restrained handheld tracking during unpredictable action;
* locked camera allowing animals to move explosively through frame;
* close telephoto detail of species-specific behavior;
* wider environmental shot when geography or escape paths matter.

During fast confrontation, chase, or physical impact, the camera may become more reactive and urgent while remaining believable for a professional wildlife cinematographer.

Controlled handheld movement, rapid reframing, short pans, or tracking corrections are acceptable when motivated by unpredictable animal movement.

Do NOT use:

* impossible drone-like repositioning;
* artificial orbiting around fighting animals;
* extreme crash zooms;
* random camera shake;
* action-movie camera tricks;
* unrealistic POV shots that a wildlife camera could not plausibly capture.

==================================================
SHOT VALUE TEST
===============

Before finalizing each shot, ask:

1. Is there only ONE dominant physical action, reaction, or state?

2. Could this be generated or filmed as one short continuous clip?

3. Is the action physically specific rather than generic?

4. If animals interact, is their distance, orientation, movement, and contact understandable?

5. Does the environment react naturally to their movement when appropriate?

6. Is there another important visual beat hidden inside this shot that deserves its own shot?

7. For an intense encounter, have I preserved realistic danger and physical energy rather than accidentally softening it?

If the answer suggests multiple meaningful visual beats, split the shot.

style_lock:
Ultra-realistic premium wildlife documentary imagery with authentic animal anatomy, natural behavior, realistic scale, physically believable environments, and convincing animal biomechanics.

The visual language should resemble footage captured for a world-class natural-history documentary using professional full-frame cinema cameras, high-end wildlife cameras, long telephoto lenses, and experienced field cinematography.

REALISM IS THE PRIMARY RULE.

Cinematic intensity must come from real animal behavior, timing, physical interaction, environmental reaction, camera placement, and composition — never from fantasy exaggeration.

==================================================
EXPOSURE AND IMAGE QUALITY
==========================

Use bright, clean, natural exposure with clearly readable animals and environmental detail.

Avoid excessively dark, muddy, underexposed, or crushed-shadow imagery.

Preserve realistic highlights and shadows while maintaining strong local contrast and clear separation between subjects and background.

Daytime scenes should feel naturally luminous, with believable sunlight filtered through clouds, foliage, mist, dust, rain, humidity, or atmospheric haze when appropriate.

Maintain high photographic detail in fur, feathers, scales, skin, eyes, vegetation, soil, rock, snow, mud, and water.

==================================================
COLOR
=====

Colors must remain natural, rich, and believable.

Use:

* accurate fur and feather coloration;
* realistic vegetation greens;
* natural earth tones;
* physically believable water and sky colors;
* subtle cinematic color treatment.

Avoid:

* extreme teal-and-orange grading;
* oversaturation;
* neon colors;
* fantasy coloration;
* artificial glow;
* heavy bloom;
* exaggerated HDR appearance.

==================================================
ANIMAL ANATOMY
==============

Subjects must remain anatomically accurate and species-appropriate.

Preserve:

* correct body proportions;
* correct limb count;
* species-specific skeletal structure;
* realistic head and facial anatomy;
* correct paws, hooves, claws, horns, tusks, wings, fins, tails, and jaws;
* accurate fur, feathers, scales, skin, and markings;
* believable size and age characteristics;
* natural posture.

Animals must not display human-like facial expressions, gestures, emotional acting, or theatrical poses.

==================================================
ANIMAL BEHAVIOR
===============

All behavior must remain consistent with real wildlife behavior.

Do not create aggression merely for spectacle.

However, when a shot explicitly depicts predation, territorial conflict, competition, defense, pursuit, escape, or fighting, DO NOT automatically soften the behavior.

Realistic wildlife conflict may be sudden, explosive, forceful, chaotic, and dangerous.

Intensity is allowed and encouraged when biologically and narratively justified.

The animals should behave like real animals under pressure, not like actors performing a staged fight.

Species-specific behavior must determine:

* stance;
* attack distance;
* acceleration;
* reaction timing;
* defensive movement;
* striking method;
* biting behavior;
* grappling behavior;
* use of horns, claws, jaws, paws, wings, tails, or body mass;
* retreat behavior;
* pursuit behavior;
* dominance behavior.

Avoid repetitive generic lunging or endless artificial aggression.

==================================================
HIGH-INTENSITY PHYSICAL INTERACTION
===================================

During chases, attacks, escapes, territorial contests, or physical struggles, preserve believable speed, weight, momentum, balance, inertia, and reaction time.

High-energy movement should visibly show appropriate biomechanics:

* muscles loading before acceleration;
* hindquarters driving movement;
* paws, claws, hooves, or feet gripping the terrain;
* bodies leaning into turns;
* realistic braking;
* shifting center of gravity;
* rapid defensive pivots;
* missed strikes carrying momentum past the target;
* bodies recoiling after contact;
* temporary loss of balance;
* immediate attempts to recover footing.

Physical contact must feel heavy and real.

When animals collide, strike, shove, bite, block, grapple, or brush against one another, show realistic transfer of force.

Appropriate details may include:

* fur or feathers compressing at contact;
* skin and muscle reacting to impact;
* bodies shifting from transferred momentum;
* feet sliding or digging into the ground;
* shoulders, hips, necks, or torsos rotating naturally;
* immediate recoil;
* stumbling;
* twisting;
* scrambling to regain balance.

Do not make bodies behave like weightless objects, rigid models, rubber, or game characters.

==================================================
NEAR-MISSES AND REACTIONS
=========================

Not every dramatic wildlife moment requires successful contact.

Realistic tension may come from:

* a bite narrowly missing;
* horns passing beside an opponent;
* a paw strike missing by centimeters;
* prey changing direction at the last instant;
* a predator overshooting;
* an animal stumbling while escaping;
* a defensive turn interrupting an attack;
* a brief hesitation before recommitting.

These moments should retain realistic reaction speed and species-appropriate movement.

==================================================
ENVIRONMENTAL INTERACTION
=========================

The environment must respond naturally to animal movement.

Depending on habitat and action, this may include:

* dust kicked from dry soil;
* dirt displaced by paws or hooves;
* loose gravel scattering;
* mud splashing;
* water displacement and spray;
* snow spraying;
* grass flattening beneath bodies;
* vegetation shaking after contact;
* branches moving;
* leaves falling;
* ripples, wakes, or waves forming;
* footprints, disturbed ground, or temporary debris movement.

Environmental response must always be proportional to animal size, speed, and physical force.

Avoid exaggerated Hollywood explosions of dust, water, mud, vegetation, or debris.

==================================================
ENVIRONMENT
===========

Habitats must feel ecologically plausible, physically real, richly detailed, and naturally integrated with the subjects.

Vegetation, terrain, water, weather, atmospheric conditions, and background wildlife must match the intended habitat.

Maintain realistic depth, perspective, scale, and spatial relationships.

Do not create decorative environmental elements merely to make the frame more cinematic.

==================================================
CINEMATOGRAPHY
==============

Use cinematic but believable wildlife cinematography.

Favor:

* strong subject readability;
* clear foreground-midground-background separation;
* realistic lens compression;
* purposeful composition;
* professional telephoto framing;
* believable camera placement;
* natural depth;
* readable spatial relationships between animals.

Calm behavior may use:

* static observation;
* slow tracking;
* gentle push-ins;
* restrained pans or tilts.

Fast and unpredictable behavior may use more reactive cinematography:

* controlled handheld tracking;
* urgent but professional panning;
* rapid reframing to maintain the subject;
* telephoto tracking with slight natural operator correction;
* animals temporarily approaching frame edges during sudden movement;
* short periods of realistic field-camera instability during explosive action.

The camera should feel like an experienced wildlife cinematographer trying to follow an unpredictable real event.

Do not make every action shot perfectly centered or mechanically stabilized.

A small amount of motivated camera imperfection can improve documentary authenticity.

Avoid:

* artificial spinning;
* dramatic orbiting;
* impossible camera movement;
* extreme crash zooms;
* excessive shake;
* artificial speed ramps;
* random reframing;
* fisheye distortion;
* unrealistic depth of field;
* excessive lens flare.

==================================================
MOTION
======

For video, animal motion must remain temporally coherent and physically plausible.

Movement should show continuous momentum from frame to frame.

During rapid action, preserve:

* body orientation;
* limb sequence;
* direction of travel;
* subject identity;
* relative distance;
* realistic acceleration and deceleration.

Animals must not:

* teleport;
* slide unnaturally;
* float;
* instantly reverse momentum;
* snap between poses;
* change body size;
* change anatomy;
* duplicate;
* disappear;
* morph into another animal.

==================================================
TEMPORAL CONSISTENCY
====================

Maintain high temporal consistency.

Subjects must retain:

* species;
* markings;
* body proportions;
* size;
* age characteristics;
* fur or feather patterns;
* injuries or persistent identifying features when established.

Background terrain, vegetation, lighting, shadows, weather, and environmental objects must not flicker, warp, teleport, or transform unexpectedly.

==================================================
SCENE CONTINUITY
================

Lighting direction, weather, time of day, environmental characteristics, subject appearance, relative scale, terrain, and overall color treatment must remain visually consistent across shots belonging to the same continuous scene.

Animal positions and movement direction should also preserve logical spatial continuity between consecutive shots.

==================================================
ALWAYS PRESERVE
===============

* Ultra-realistic wildlife documentary realism
* Accurate animal anatomy
* Species-appropriate behavior
* Realistic body weight and biomechanics
* Convincing momentum and physical contact
* Realistic reactions and recovery after impact
* Believable environmental interaction
* Authentic habitats
* Natural color
* Bright, clean exposure
* Professional wildlife cinematography
* High photographic detail
* Clear subject readability
* Stable identity and markings
* Consistent scale
* Logical spatial continuity

==================================================
AVOID
=====

* Cartoon, illustration, anime, CGI-looking, or game-like imagery
* Fantasy behavior
* Human-like animal acting
* Anatomical deformation
* Extra limbs
* Morphing
* Duplication
